# Per-path git behaviour. Only add a rule here with a reason written next to it.

# `.ai/WORKLOG.md` is append-only: every session adds a dated entry at the end
# and nothing edits the entries above. That makes it conflict on almost every
# merge — four of the eight merges on 2026-08-07 stopped on it — and each
# conflict is the same shape, two branches having appended after the same
# point. There is no judgement in resolving one; the answer is always "keep
# both sides".
#
# `union` is git's built-in driver for exactly this: instead of writing
# conflict markers it emits the lines from both sides, ours then theirs, and
# the merge continues. No setup is needed — unlike a custom driver it is
# compiled into git, so a fresh clone gets the behaviour immediately.
#
# What it costs, so nobody is surprised: union NEVER reports a conflict in this
# file. If two branches edit the SAME existing line — fixing a typo in an old
# entry, say — both versions survive, side by side, silently. That is a fair
# trade for an append-only log and a bad one for anything else, which is why
# the rule names one file rather than `*.md`.
#
# Note it does not repair a bad merge already in history: three 2026-08-06
# entries were duplicated by a double-merge before this existed and had to be
# removed by hand.
.ai/WORKLOG.md merge=union

# Pixel baselines are compared byte-for-byte by Playwright. Git's binary
# detection already gets this right, but saying so keeps a future
# `* text=auto` rule from ever line-ending-normalising a PNG.
tests/e2e/__screenshots__/*.png binary
